File: `labels.js`
```javascript
exports.label = function(text) {
  return "Label: " + text;
};
```
This is the real function. `exports.label` makes it available to another file using CommonJS, a way to share code between files.

File: `main.ts`
```typescript
import { label } from "./labels";
console.log(label("Stars"));
```
This uses the function and prints `Label: Stars`. TypeScript reads its description from `labels.d.ts`. Node, the program that runs JavaScript on your computer, needs the real `labels.js`.
